--TEST--
`Core\Response::sendFile` refuses at the call what it cannot send — a name that is not there and a directory — and sends the file beside them, each bound named on both sides — `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`, spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Everything here runs with the grant in place, so the door is not what
// answers and the case is about where the member stops. Each pair is one bound
// named on both sides -- the thing refused beside the thing sent -- because a
// member that refused one step too early prints plausibly against either half
// on its own.

// Absence. The refusal is at the *call*, which is the point of asking here
// rather than at the connection: the handler learns that the file it named is
// not there while it can still answer something else, instead of from a `500`
// after it returned.
try {
    Core\Response::sendFile("missing.bin");
    echo "missing.bin: sent\n";
} catch (IOError $absent) {
    echo "missing.bin: no file\n";
}

// Kind. A directory is granted, resolvable and readable, and it is still not a
// body: a response carries a file's contents, and a directory has none. The
// message is printed whole rather than a label of this case's own, because what
// a refusal says is the part of it a handler's author reads.
try {
    Core\Response::sendFile("sub");
    echo "sub: sent\n";
} catch (LogicError $wrong) {
    echo $wrong->message, "\n";
}

// The other side of both bounds: a regular file inside that same directory,
// sent whole. Off a request the bytes land in this program's output, which is
// what makes the accepted half assertable beside the refused ones.
Core\Response::sendFile("sub/keep.txt");
--FILE sub/keep.txt--
kept
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
--EXPECTF--
missing.bin: no file
Core\Response::sendFile(): `%s%esub` is not a regular file — a response body is a file's contents, and a directory has none to send
kept
